I think you’ve bundled together several deep questions that are worth separating because some are well-supported by physics, while others are intriguing philosophical hypotheses.

The strongest idea is this:

Many properties are fundamentally relational rather than intrinsic.

Modern physics already contains many examples of this.

* Velocity is only meaningful relative to a reference frame.
* Electric potential is measured relative to another point.
* Curvature is about relationships between nearby points in spacetime.
* Networks derive behavior from connectivity as much as from individual nodes.
* Biology depends heavily on interactions among cells, molecules, and environments rather than isolated components.

So the intuition that “relationships matter” is well grounded.

⸻

The next step you’re taking is more ambitious:

Geometry gives rise to behavior.

That’s also a productive engineering lens.

A bridge behaves differently because of its geometry.

Proteins fold into specific geometries that determine function.

Crystal lattices produce different material properties.

Neural network architectures affect learning capability.

The behavior emerges from the structure and the governing rules acting on that structure.

One could summarize that as

\text{Behavior}
=
f(\text{Relations},\ \text{Constraints},\ \text{Dynamics})

Notice that geometry alone isn’t sufficient; the governing dynamics matter too.

